[Music]  you're watching the daily climate show  on sky news coming up a cabinet meeting  to find solutions to the cost of living  crisis becomes a row over net zero  we're policy too much coal to slow  climate change a stark warning from a  new report  and climate change or corruption what's  to blame for the devastating floods in  south africa  hello and welcome to the uk's only daily  climate news show where we track the  changes happening to our world hear from  the people being hit hardest by those  changes and report on the race for  solutions  around has broken out amongst cabinet  ministers over how best to deal with the  uk's cost of living crisis with one  going so far as the call for an overhaul  of net zero targets well let's get more  on this we can speak to sky's deputy  political editor sam code so sam what  are you hearing  britain may have legislated to implement  net zero by 2050. it may have hosted the  cop climate change summit in glasgow but  the basics of that target are still  being fought over in cabinet that  happened earlier today prime minister  boris johnson asked cabinet ministers to  bring ideas to cut people's bills  because the cost of living crisis uh at  this morning's uh cabinet and it quickly  descended into a row over net zero uh  the policing minister kit milhouse  kicked off suggesting that tax cuts were  the answer jacob reese mogg chipped in  that he agreed but he was the one that  once again put those net zero targets on  the agenda at which point quasi quatting  the business secretary whose brief  overseas uh environmental targets uh  came in and defended them he's also gone  public with his defense of net zero  saying on twitter uk  gas generation in april 2022 is around  four times more expensive to run the new  solar or wind farm so mounting a public  defense there another environmental  policy related policy discussed in  cabinet today maybe extending the amount  of time cars can take before having an  mot from one year to two critics suggest  that could mean more dirty cars  potentially more dangerous cars on the  road as a result all of this underscores  how difficult it is uh to tackle the  cost of living crisis and how easy it is  for critics to pick on the net zero  target as a potential area for cost  savings  sam thank you  now too much coal is being burned for  the world to stay within safe  temperature limits that's the conclusion  of a report by the global energy monitor  published today  coal is the biggest single contributor  to global greenhouse emissions so  reducing its use is essential if the  world is to have any hope of limiting  global warming to 1.5 degrees but there  is some good news our science  correspondent thomas more is here to  explain thomas  cole is the dirtiest of all the fossil  fuels the one the world needs to abandon  most rapidly to avoid catastrophic  climate change so how are we doing well  according to a new report by global  energy monitor total coal power capacity  under development fell by 13 last year  down from 525 gigawatts to 457 to reach  a record low but that's where the good  news ends the report shows 34 countries  still had new coal plants in the  pipeline and the operating capacity of  coal plants grew by more than 18  gigawatts as countries delayed plans to  retire power stations because of the  post-covered economic rebound and the  supply crunch in natural gas remember  that was the year of the glasgow climate  pact in which countries agreed to phase  out coal and the urgency was highlighted  by the recent ipcc report by un  scientists to keep the global  temperature rise below the critical 1.5  degrees they said there can be no new  coal plants and coal use needs to fall  by 75 by 2030. we are way off that  target much of the growth in coal was in  china with just over  gigawatts of newly commissioned capacity  as factories opened up again nearly  offsetting all the coal plant  retirements in the rest of the world  and the u.s also slowed its shutdown of  coal plants in 2019 just over 16  gigawatts of coal power was retired in  2021 the estimated switch-off was  between 6.4 and 9. the trend in europe  was more encouraging but the record 12.9  gigawatts of capacity retired in 2021.  now all these figures predate the war in  ukraine that has turbo charged the price  of energy this spring not just oil and  gas but coal too spiked as russia  invaded the eu has said countries could  continue to burn coal as an alternative  to russian gas that's more greenhouse  gas emissions of course and global  energy monitor warns the world needs to  do much more much faster to wean itself  off coal  thomas thanks very much indeed  so let's get some of the day's other  climate news now and environmental  charities have called for the government  to reject plans for the uk's first new  coal mine in over 30 years communities  and local government secretary michael  gove is considering west cumbria  mining's application for a new mine near  whitehaven  14 organizations including greenpeace  have written to mr gove and urged him to  reject the plans saying hundreds of  green jobs in solar power offshore wind  and low carbon heating could be created  instead  the uk government is being urged to  provide at least 900 million pounds to  help stop a famine in east africa oxfam  warns that up to 28 million people are  facing severe hunger in parts of  ethiopia kenya and somalia following a  drought caused by consecutive poor  rainfall seasons the charity says the uk  funding is only a third of what it was  in 2017 with just 288 million pounds  allocated to the countries over the last  year  a brazilian federal court has upheld the  suspension of an environmental license  for what was planned to be the largest  open-pit gold mine in the amazon  rainforest  previously the court ruled that bello  sun mining's consultation with local  indigenous people didn't meet the  criteria required by the national indian  foundation  climate campaigners have warned the mine  would cause significant deforestation  and contaminate nearby rivers which  could threaten local communities  the south african president is sounding  the alarm on the climate crisis more  than 400 people died and more are still  missing after torrential rain led to  flooding and landslides in kwazulu-natal  province the city of durban the  country's largest port and its informal  settlements took the brunt of the  flooding after the heaviest rainfall in  the region in more than 60 years  today in an address to parliament  president ramaphosa warned that the  effects of climate change are now being  felt and the country needs to get  serious about climate adaptation  these storms honorable members  resulting losses of life and the  destruction of key infrastructure  are a reminder that climate change  remains the biggest single threat  to the future of humanity  the south african weather service notes  that extreme weather events  are more prevalent in recent times  across most regions of the country  the effects of climate change are  already being  widely felt  as we rebuild from this disaster  we need to ensure that we are guided  by the principles and approaches  contained  in the adaptation strategy  well joining me now is dr tafadzua  mapaudi professor in climate water and  food systems at the university of  kwazulu-natal  welcome to you first of all do you agree  with the president there that extreme  weather that's being experienced is down  to climate change  uh yes we  we have been experiencing an increasing  frequency  and intensity in weather extremes bought  floods and droughts  in the last couple of years so that's  that's quite accurate  and what about  what this is down to there's been an  extreme loss of life as an extreme  number of buildings damaged as well  is this because those buildings were  inadequately built in the first place do  you think or  simply built in the wrong places  i think the you know the impact of these  floods exposes certain things  mainly the inequality in in south africa  that still persists years after the end  of apartheid  and the fact that a lot of poor people  are still residing  within informal settlements that are  mostly located in you know flood risk  areas and on steep slopes  so naturally those people would be at  risk if there's a flood where they're in  a low lying area landslides if you're on  a steep slope and because they're  settled in formal areas  uh another thing is naturally you know  the the construction of the house is  it's using cheap materials there are  poor people  there's no proper planning there's no  proper retention for slobs  you know the design and management of  slopes so it speaks a lot to the issue  of poverty and inequality that still  persists  which puts people at risk  well yes and cyril ramaphosa is saying  climate change is serious it's here  given that the country is already  suffering from climate change how much  focus is there at the moment on  adaptation  i think  there is a focus on adaptation but it's  clearly been underfunded in recent years  the amount of money that has been you  know channeled specifically towards  climate change adaptation  is is very minimal  and i think it's a dilemma that a lot of  developing countries find themselves in  that are still trying to find economic  development and at the same time we are  having to  now adapt to climate change so that  balance between economic development job  creation alleviating poverty  and then you know climate change  adaptation  uh you know the trade offs in that space  that's one of the challenges that is  there so the serious need to increase  the amount of funding going into climate  change adaptation and also to really  look at the mega projects infrastructure  projects that are being done to ensure  that you know those are planned with  climate change in mind  they are climate resilient and also to  really look a whole lot of other  supporting sectors in the economy that  are sensitive to climate change and that  are vulnerable to it  dr  mapaudi thanks very much indeed  coming up i'll be joined by chief  executive of the climate group helen  clarkson and morgan gillespie from the  food and land use coalition and we're  going to be discussing some long-term  solutions to food insecurity and extreme  weather events brought about by climate  change  [Music]  so  [Music]  i'm david blevins and i'm skye's senior  ireland correspondent  [Music]  i'm based in belfast a city transformed  by peace but still struggling with its  past  politically the two sides are as far  apart as ever and it's over that  question of who has sovereignty in  northern ireland the very issue that's  been the source of tension here for  generations  it takes 60 minutes to cross the irish  sea it took the british monarchy  essentially  i've spent 25 years reporting the  journey from conflict to peace  the political parties of northern  ireland have reached agreement  we help you understand the world with us  today we have reached an agreement with  the conservative party on support for  government in parliament  we already know what the government gets  out of this deal what exactly does the  dup get out of it well 1.5 billion  pounds  welcome to winterfell it's an iconic  location in this globally acclaimed  drama  ophelia was the most powerful hurricane  this far east in the atlantic on record  you can't live in a place like this and  not appreciate the environment  hello and welcome back to the daily  climate show on sky news and let's get  straight to some of those climate  stories that we've heard about with  helen clarkson chief executive of the  climate group and morgan gillespie from  the food and land use coalition so  welcome to both of you nice to see you  both  so years of drought and poor harvests  are threatening serious famine across  east africa oxfam has called on the uk  government to help with direct funding  to alleviate the crisis  but in the face of ever worse drought  and with added pressure on food security  from the ukraine war what are the  solutions so morgan to you first of all  east africa then suffering drought so  first of all what impact is climate  change having on food production in  africa  so food prices are surging around the  world they're in record highs in many  countries now this is due not only to  climate change but to conflict such as  the ukraine crisis but also covid  right the consequences caused by the war  in the ukraine are particularly acute  for low-income countries that are  reliant on food and warts such as wheat  or as fertilizer from ukraine and russia  and that includes many countries in  sub-saharan africa many of which have  already seen an increase in malnutrition  rates over recent years so this is  compounding an already quite serious  problem  well yes so helen we've got the problem  of climate change  really causing challenges for  for farmers across places like east  africa but we've also got the war in  ukraine adding complications in there as  well because  of the the problem with the imports of  grains but also fertilizer as well  yeah and i think what we're seeing here  is you know all these uh problems piling  on top of each other and this is what  climate activists have been saying for a  long time in the sense that you've got  all these problems in the world and then  you put climate on top of them or  underneath them and it sort of makes  them even worse and that's why we really  need to think about resilience in the  round and understand that by going after  these things individually you know  that's where missing opportunities to  build a much more resilient future for  everyone okay well let's try and  map out what that resilient future looks  like morgan what would your solutions be  for for local people to try to make sure  that they can boost their productivity  locally  well i mean i think that there's things  that need to happen at multiple levels  right so governments have a role to play  financial institutions have a role to  play trade organization businesses but  there's like five or six things that can  happen pretty urgently that will help  uh mitigate this food crisis so number  one we have to mainta maintain open  flows of trade every nation depends on  this for their food supplies nations  can't start stockpiling food or  resources  number two we need to increase food  production but we need to do this  mindfully and appropriately in different  regions of the world and we need to do  it sustainably and without delay and  that can be in response to the shortages  that we're seeing from the war in  ukraine now what about finance we need  to give the poorest communities around  the world safety nuts that they need to  get through this crisis and that  includes countries in africa and the  middle east and we also need to support  countries again in particular in africa  with the financing that they need for  immediate food supplies right the safety  nets to address the needs of the poor  for small farmers facing higher input  prices  now what else can we do kind of outside  of the global north we think about  things such as relaxing or even  eliminating biofuel mandates at least  temporarily at a time when we're seeing  more people facing hunger the world's  cropland should be used to grow food  not fuel and we really need to avoid  efforts  made to quickly and unsustainably  convert natural land into farmland and  instead concentrate production increases  on already productive lands we don't  want to start destroying nature  destroying the environment to grow new  crops we want to instead really focus on  improving yields on existing  productivity okay  helen what do you make of of those  solutions mapped out and how easy  easy is it to take that that broad of  you when you're in a country that's  suffering right now from famine  i think this is the difficulty and i  think this is where the world's  community needs to kind of come together  and understand and work together on  these long-term solutions and i think  you know kind of broadening out even  from that if we look at what's happening  with the crisis in ukraine you can see  lots of countries and you know we heard  earlier in your report about you know  the uk cabinet meeting today and someone  wanting to jettison these long-term  plans we're not good at long-term  planning we're not good at understanding  complex interconnecting crises and i  think we've got a lot of advice coming  out from our sector and others and what  we need is is governments to sort of  really shift their mindset onto that  long term because the solutions like the  ones that morgan set out are there but  they are complex to implement and  they're going to need real cohesion from  the global community  okay let's move on because the um  massive floods in south africa have left  hundreds of people dead and of course  serious damage we heard a little bit  earlier from president cyril ramaphosa  he's not the only person to attribute  the devastation to climate change but  also poor infrastructure and lack of  investment  shame the blame as well so where does  the balance lie between mitigation  reducing emissions and adaptation to the  effects which are already with us  morgan given that you have got a lot of  people saying that  floods that we're seeing in south africa  are down to climate change given that  climate change is happening now is there  enough focus on adaptation in areas like  that  all right i actually don't think so i  don't think there's enough focus on  adaptation there's certainly not enough  climate finance being pushed towards  adaptation and again it's not that we  don't know the solutions but we're  lacking perhaps the political will the  political might and certainly the  financing to implement those solutions  to help these countries that are already  being  quite clearly impacted in devastating  ways  well yes and helen  clearly preventing climate change is  seen as a huge priority but is the  balance right between the focus on that  and the focus on adapting in areas where  climate change is already affecting  people  yeah i mean i i think we're beyond the  point where we've got a choice between  adaptation  and mitigation or even that they're two  different things now we've got to adapt  to climate change it's here it's  happening we're seeing catastrophes like  the one uh now in south africa if you  look at the figures i've seen un figures  that show that um sort of from 1970 to  2000 the world experienced sort of 90 to  100  medium to large disasters a year in 2015  that number had gone up to 400 and by  2030 we're expecting over 550 that's a  huge difference in what the world needs  to be dealing with so the idea that we  can you know not adapt or understand  that impact isn't there but mitigation  is an important contributor to  adaptation because if we if we want  fewer catastrophes to happen we need to  limit uh the carbon going into the  atmosphere now and try and pull back as  close as we can to that 1.5 degree  target so we need to be doing everything  and i think people  think you know oh we can choose this or  choose that but actually we've really  got to put climate at the heart of all  our decisions now whether those are  infrastructure decisions on you know the  way that you build infrastructure and  the materials you use and how do you  move towards  zero carbon infrastructure the  accelerating the energy transition as  rapidly as possible all of these things  kind of come together to make a more  resilient world where we limit warming  as far as we can and that and then set  ourselves up for surviving that world  that we've created and morgan when you  come to the adaptation part of that it  costs money doesn't it building flood  defenses costs money doesn't it and the  poorer nations would say we contributed  the least to climate change we tend to  be suffering the most and the polluters  ought to pay for it so is that happening  is enough money giving through to the  people that need it  no absolutely not there's not enough  money in the system there's not enough  money being pushed towards these  countries we saw that as an outcome of  cop 26. the big gaps are still around  financing and that's not to say that  other things can't be done you know  there are solutions out there that can  be implemented now one of those things  uh the sort of links to what helen was  talking to is thinking about nature  based solutions particularly within food  and land use systems and they're they're  simple to say but they are hard to  implement but you know we can do things  like just stop deforestation we could  halt deforestation today and that would  make a significant difference in  mitigating emissions and developing  greater sinks to increase our carbon  storage capabilities  linked to that we can also adopt more  productive and regenerative land use  practices right so adapting to climate  change by leveraging new farming  techniques that enable us to produce  more crops on less land and more  regenerative ways that we're protecting  biodiversity protecting fresh water  resources and some of these new  techniques don't cost lots of money  right  you can kind of support farmers in less  expensive ways to start implementing  these now and as helen said we kind of  have to throw all the tools that we have  in the toolkit towards this problem we  really don't have much time to lose okay  and helen we don't have much time  actually left on the program either so  very briefly is the biggest problem  money when it comes to adaptation or a  government's just too short term  i think it's both and i think you know  we've seen the right commitments we're  way forward on where we were paris five  years ago on what governments are  willing to say but now they need the  will to act right away and i think we're  seeing a bit too much we'll look at our  net zero target but we'll just do this  one last bad thing and we we just don't  have time now for one last coal mine or  one last fossil fuel investment we need  to take those targets that governments  have set and make them a reality and  that needs money and it needs will lots  of ideas and solutions uh helen and  morgan very interesting to talk to you  both thanks very much indeed  thank you  so coming up after the roundabout  newspaper comments about angela rayner  trevor phillips and guests will discuss  whether misogyny should be made a hate  crime that's on common ground which is  up next but that's it from me thanks for  watching and i'll see you next time  [Music]  [Music]  you

